{"timestamp": "2026-08-04T21:29:13.419611", "data": {"url": "https://shapes.inc/fandom/marvel-comics/timeline", "title": "Marvel Comics Timeline \u2014 Complete Chronology | Shapes", "content": "shapes inc Marvel Comics Overview Characters Timeline Quotes Episodes Ships Controversies Deep Dives Author / Creators Multiverse And Realities Marvel Comics Timeline A comprehensive chronological guide to the Marvel Universe, spanning from the birth of the Multiverse to the modern era of Blood Hunt and the rise of Emperor Doom. Explore Marvel Comics characters Tony Stark Steve Rogers Peter Parker Thor Odinson Bruce Banner Natasha Romanoff Victor von Doom Logan William Kaplan Clinton Francis Barton Edward Charles Allan Brock Erik Lehnsherr Forge Ororo Munroe Scott Summers Theodore Altman Venom Symbiote Chat with other Pop Culture fans Billions of years ago (In-Universe) The First Firmament, the original sentient universe, is shattered during a war between its creations, the Aspirants and the Celestials. This cataclysmic event gives birth to the Multiverse and the first iteration of the Eternity entity. This event establishes the cosmic hierarchy and the cycle of renewal for the Marvel Multiverse. October 1939 (Real-World) Timely Comics publishes Marvel Comics #1, featuring the first appearances of the Human Torch (Jim Hammond) and Namor the Sub-Mariner. This marks the official birth of the Marvel publishing line under Martin Goodman. It introduced the concept of the anti-hero through Namor and laid the foundation for the Marvel brand. 1941 (In-Universe) Steve Rogers undergoes the Super-Soldier procedure under the supervision of Abraham Erskine at a secret facility in New Jersey. He becomes Captain America , the Sentinel of Liberty , to combat the threat of the Red Skull and the Axis powers. This created the moral compass of the Marvel Universe and the archetype for the modern superhero. November 1961 (Real-World) Stan Lee and Jack Kirby release Fantastic Four #1, launching the \"Marvel Age of Comics.\" Reed Richards, Sue Storm, Johnny Storm, and Ben Grimm gain powers after exposure to cosmic rays, introducing flawed, humanized heroes to the medium. This issue shifted the industry toward character-driven storytelling and interconnected continuity. August 1962 (Real-World) Amazing Fantasy #15 debuts Peter Parker , a teenage protagonist burdened by the death of his Uncle Ben. Created by Stan Lee and Steve Ditko, Spider-Man becomes the face of Marvel Comics globally. It proved that younger characters could be leads rather than sidekicks, revolutionizing reader demographics. September 1963 (Real-World) The Avengers #1 and X-Men #1 are both published, introducing the \"Earth's Mightiest Heroes\" and the \"Children of the Atom.\" While the Avengers represent sanctioned heroism, the X-Men introduce the theme of social prejudice through the mutant metaphor. These two teams would become the primary pillars of Marvel's team-based storytelling for decades. June 1973 (Real-World) In The Amazing Spider-Man #121, Gwen Stacy is killed during a battle between Spider-Man and the Green Goblin at the George Washington Bridge. This event is widely considered the end of the Silver Age of Comics. It established that major supporting characters were not safe, bringing a new level of maturity and consequence to the medium. 1980 (Real-World) The Dark Phoenix Saga concludes in Uncanny X-Men #137, where Jean Grey commits suicide on the Moon to prevent the Phoenix Force from consuming the universe. This Chris Claremont and John Byrne collaboration remains a definitive mutant narrative. It set the standard for cosmic-scale tragedy and the moral complexity of the X-Men franchise. 1984 \u2013 1985 (Real-World) The Beyonder teleports Earth's greatest heroes and villains to Battleworld for the first Secret Wars. This 12-issue limited series was the first major company-wide crossover event in Marvel history. It introduced Spider-Man 's black suit ( the Symbiote ) and established the \"event comic\" business model. 1991 (Real-World) The Infinity Gauntlet miniseries by Jim Starlin features Thanos erasing half of all life in the universe with a snap of his fingers. The remaining heroes launch a desperate assault on his space temple to recover the Infinity Gems. This solidified Thanos as the premier cosmic threat of the Marvel Universe. 2004 \u2013 2005 (In-Universe) Wanda Maximoff suffers a mental breakdown in Avengers Disassembled, leading to the deaths of Hawkeye, Ant-Man, and Vision. This culminates in House of M, where she utters the words \"No more mutants,\" depowering 90% of the mutant population. This decimated the mutant race for years and led to the formation of the New Avengers. 2006 \u2013 2007 (In-Universe) The Superhuman Registration Act is passed following the Stamford tragedy, sparking a Civil War between Iron Man and Captain America . The conflict ends with the surrender of Steve Rogers and his subsequent assassination. It fractured the hero community and led to the \"Dark Reign\" era where villains took control of national security. 2012 (In-Universe) The Phoenix Force returns to Earth in Avengers vs. X-Men, causing a war between the two factions over the fate of Hope Summers. Cyclops kills Charles Xavier while possessed by the Phoenix, fundamentally changing his character into a revolutionary figure. The event ended the \"No More Mutants\" era and reignited the mutant race. 2015 (In-Universe) The Multiverse is destroyed by Incursions, leading to the final Secret Wars. Doctor Doom uses the power of the Beyonders to create a new Battleworld, ruling as God Emperor until Reed Richards restores the Prime Marvel Universe (Earth-616). This served as a soft reboot that integrated characters like Miles Morales into the main continuity. 2019 (In-Universe) Charles Xavier, Magneto, and Moira MacTaggert establish a sovereign mutant nation on the living island of Krakoa. This \"House of X\" era introduces mutant resurrection protocols and a new isolationist foreign policy for mutantkind. It was the most radical status quo shift for the X-Men in thirty years. 2023 \u2013 2024 (In-Universe) The Fall of X sees the destruction of the Krakoan dream by the anti-mutant organization Orchis. This leads to the \"Rise of the Powers of X\" and the eventual transition into the \"From the Ashes\" era, where mutants return to living among humanity. This marked the end of the Krakoan age and a return to the classic \"outlaw\" status of the X-Men. May \u2013 August 2024 (Real-World) The Blood Hunt event, written by Jed MacKay, depicts a global vampire invasion led by Varnae, who possesses the body of Blade. The world is plunged into eternal darkness as the Avengers and Doctor Strange fight to reclaim the light. The event concludes with Doctor Doom becoming the Sorcerer Supreme after Stephen Strange abdicates his mantle to save the world. August \u2013 October 2024 (Real-World) The Venom War erupts as Eddie Brock and his son Dylan Brock fight for control over the Venom symbiote . Both have seen different futures where the other leads the world to ruin, leading to a massive conflict involving the \"Zombiotes.\" It redefines the relationship between the Brocks and the nature of the King in Black . Early 2025 (In-Universe) The \"One World Under Doom\" initiative begins, following Doctor Doom 's ascension to Sorcerer Supreme. Doom uses his combined mastery of science and magic to impose global order, forcing the Avengers into a role of underground resistance. This establishes Victor von Doom as the central antagonist and de facto ruler of the Marvel Universe heading into 2026. Overview The definitive guide to Marvel Comics, exploring its vast multiverse, iconic characters like Spider-Man and the Avengers, and its evolution from Timely Publications to a global cultural phenomenon. Characters A comprehensive guide to the definitive icons of the Marvel Comics universe, detailing the origins, powers, and interconnected relationships of Earth's Mightiest Heroes and their greatest adversaries. Deep Dive An exhaustive exploration of high-level Marvel Comics concepts, ranging from the metaphysical mechanics of the One-Above-All to the socio-political implications of the Krakoan Era and the structural integrity of the Multiversal Seventh and Eighth Firmaments. Creators A comprehensive guide to the architects of the Marvel Universe, featuring the foundational legends and the modern visionaries driving the 616 and Ultimate continuities into 2026. Controversies A comprehensive overview of production scandals, narrative retcons, and editorial shifts within Marvel Comics, ranging from the Silver Age to the modern era of 2026. Episodes / Chapters Guide A definitive guide to the most influential and high-quality individual issues and story arcs in Marvel Comics history, spanning from the Silver Age to the modern era. Multiverse & Realities An exhaustive guide to the Marvel Comics Multiverse, detailing Earth-616, the Ultimate Universe, and the cosmic structures governing infinite parallel realities and dimensional planes. Ships An analytical guide to the most prominent romantic and platonic pairings within Marvel Comics, covering legendary canon romances, massive fanon ships, and the evolving landscape of queer representation in the 616 universe. Quotes A definitive collection of the most iconic and emotionally resonant quotes from Marvel Comics history, featuring legendary dialogue from Spider-Man, the X-Men, and the Avengers. Marvel Comics Creator Martin Goodman, Stan Lee, Jack Kirby, Steve Ditko Release Year 1939 (as Timely), 1961 (Modern Era) Genre Superhero, Science Fiction, Fantasy, Cosmic Horror Medium Comic Books, Graphic Novels Setting The Marvel Universe (Earth-616) and the Multiverse Episodes/Chapters 30,000+ Individual Issues Share push to ask Discover Browse Articles Engines Featured For Creators Downloads Docs Help Brand Assets Company About Research Blog Jobs Advertise Safety Legal Privacy Terms Report \u00a9 2026 Shapes, Inc. All rights reserved. \ud83c\uddfa\ud83c\uddf8 Chat with other Pop Culture fans Billions of years ago (In-Universe) The First Firmament, the original sentient universe, is shattered during a war between its creations, the Aspirants and the Celestials. This cataclysmic event gives birth to the Multiverse and the first iteration of the Eternity entity. This event establishes the cosmic hierarchy and the cycle of renewal for the Marvel Multiverse. October 1939 (Real-World) Timely Comics publishes Marvel Comics #1, featuring the first appearances of the Human Torch (Jim Hammond) and Namor the Sub-Mariner. This marks the official birth of the Marvel publishing line under Martin Goodman. It introduced the concept of the anti-hero through Namor and laid the foundation for the Marvel brand. 1941 (In-Universe) Steve Rogers undergoes the Super-Soldier procedure under the supervision of Abraham Erskine at a secret facility in New Jersey. He becomes Captain America , the Sentinel of Liberty , to combat the threat of the Red Skull and the Axis powers. This created the moral compass of the Marvel Universe and the archetype for the modern superhero. November 1961 (Real-World) Stan Lee and Jack Kirby release Fantastic Four #1, launching the \"Marvel Age of Comics.\" Reed Richards, Sue Storm, Johnny Storm, and Ben Grimm gain powers after exposure to cosmic rays, introducing flawed, humanized heroes to the medium. This issue shifted the industry toward character-driven storytelling and interconnected continuity. August 1962 (Real-World) Amazing Fantasy #15 debuts Peter Parker , a teenage protagonist burdened by the death of his Uncle Ben. Created by Stan Lee and Steve Ditko, Spider-Man becomes the face of Marvel Comics globally. It proved that younger characters could be leads rather than sidekicks, revolutionizing reader demographics. September 1963 (Real-World) The Avengers #1 and X-Men #1 are both published, introducing the \"E